>Bayes posterior mean is _calibrated_:  If we label thata_hat =
>E(theta|y), then E(theta | theta_hat) = theta_hat, where the
>expectation is taken over the joint distribution of y and theta.
>
>But Bayes posterior mean is not _unbiased_.  Unbiased is E(theta_hat |
>theta) = theta.  Bayes posterior mean can never be unbiased under this
>definition.
>
>Unbiasedness is an important principle in _measurement_, not so much in
>_estimation_.
>
>>  On May 2, 2017, at 7:32 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>
>>  Biased relative to what?  If you have the right model,
>>  Bayesian estimation using posterior means is unbiased
>>  by definition.
