That's a good point.  The convergence problems very well might have been because it was a bad idea to begin with.  I took a closer look at those plots and realized that the high variance was strongly associated with small sample sizes.

I also took your advice and tried fitting a hierarchical model on the variance components, but I'm getting a lot of divergent transitions.  Does this specification look OK?

parameters{
  real beta_b[n_b] ;

  real<lower = 0> cScale ; //Cauchy scale parameter
  real<lower = 0> sigmaP_b[n_b] ;
}
model{
    for(i in 1:n_b){
      beta_b[i] ~ normal(0, 10) ;
      sigmaP_b[i] ~ cauchy(0, cScale) ;
    }
    for(i in 1:N_){
      lr[i] ~ normal(beta_b[bioID[i]] , sigmaP_b[bioID[i]]) ;
      }
} // end model

I also tried putting a half-normal prior on the cScale parameter, but that didn't make a difference.

Any advice would be greatly appreciated!

Thanks,

Jonathon

On Tuesday, May 2, 2017 at 3:15:42 PM UTC-4, Ben B wrote:
> I'm not super familiar with modeling this, so maybe someone else can jump in. This sounds like something someone would have experience with.
>
> Probly the easiest way to diagnose if a model is neurotic is some way (without digging into the math) is to just simulate data from the thing you're trying to fit, and then trying to fit it. Like, it's easy to generate data from a distribution normal(a/b, 1.0), but then trying to estimate 'a' and 'b' separately in a model is going to be hard (cause there are many 'a's and 'b's that are gonna give you good fits).
>
> If Stan fits the simulated data easily, then the issue is probably something about the model not explaining your data very well that's causing your issues.
>
> Hopefully that helps :D
>
> Ben
>

