Hi Ben,

Thanks so much for your reply!  It is very helpful and exactly what I am trying to do.  I have one more quick question about how to subset the response vector y and the model matrix X, by the time variable.  Right now y is a vector and X is a matrix, each of length N, the full data set.  It seems like to be able to loop over time, say T (or as you wrote, D), you need to index the vector y and matrix X into separate groups.  I guess the alternative would be to pass them individually in the data step, but that seems more complicated.  I've seen indexing done over all N in the Stan manual and other examples, but not when it comes to looping over a subgroup.

Below is the set up I am currently working with per your suggestion.  From looking at the Stan manual you can subset vectors via indexing y[1:30], but since I do have an id vector in the data block, it seems like there should be an easier way.  Rather than subsetting X, I could also calculate mu=X*beta and then try and subset mu based on the id variable?  Sorry if these are silly questions, but I'm still trying to get familiar with Stan's indexing.  Any help you could provide would be much appreciated.

Thanks again,

Colin

data{
  int<lower=1> N; //total obs (L*T)
  int<lower=1> L; // total number of locations per year
  int<lower=1> T; //number of years, assume independence across years

  int<lower=1> [N] id; // vector of length N to id Year

  int<lower=1> p; //number of covariates

  vector [N] Y; // response vector
  matrix[N,p] X;// Model Matrix
  matrix [L,L] d; //distance matrix, same across all years
}

parameters {
  vector[p] beta; # coefficient
  real<lower=0> sigma; # error sd
  real<lower=0> tau; # sd spatial process
  real<lower=0> phi; # range/decay parameter
}

transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0> sigma2;
  real<lower=0> tau2;
  sigma2 = sigma^2;
  tau2 = tau^2;

}

model {

  vector[T] mu[L];  //save T vectors of means
  matrix[L,L] H; // Same Spatial Paramters for Each Year

  for (i in 1:(L-1)) {
    for (j in (i+1):L) {
      H[i,j] = tau2 * exp(-d[i,j]/ phi);
      H[j,i] = H[i,j]; // do not calculate twice, utilize symmetry
    }
  }
  for (k in 1:L) {
    H[k, k] = sigma2 + tau2; //diagonal of covariance matrix
  }

  L = cholesky_decomposition(H)

  for (t in 1:T){

    mu[t] = X[t]*beta  //how to break up model Matrix X into Years?

  }

  for (t in 1:T)

    y[t] ~ multi_normal_cholesky(mu[t], L);

  //priors

  sigma ~ cauchy(0,1);
  tau ~ cauchy(0,1);
  phi ~ gamma(0.5,0.5);
}

